# `!in ~` is `!in` then `~`: the `~` must follow directly.
mode: syntax
---- query
t | where x !in ~ ('a')
---- expected
PROGRAM@0..24
  PIPELINE@0..23
    NAME_REF@0..1
      IDENT@0..1 "t"
    WHITESPACE@1..2 " "
    PIPE@2..3 "|"
    WHITESPACE@3..4 " "
    WHERE_OP@4..23
      IDENT@4..9 "where"
      WHITESPACE@9..10 " "
      IN_EXPR@10..23
        NAME_REF@10..11
          IDENT@10..11 "x"
        WHITESPACE@11..12 " "
        NEGATED_OP@12..15 "!in"
        WHITESPACE@15..16 " "
        TILDE@16..17 "~"
        WHITESPACE@17..18 " "
        ARG_LIST@18..23
          L_PAREN@18..19 "("
          LITERAL@19..22
            STRING@19..22 "'a'"
          R_PAREN@22..23 ")"
  WHITESPACE@23..24 "\n"

error[DS0129]: `!in~` is written without a space
  --> query:1:13
  |
1 | t | where x !in ~ ('a')
  |             ^^^^^ read as `!in` followed by `~`
  |
  = help: write `!in~` to match the list ignoring case

with the machine-applicable fixes applied:
    t | where x !in~ ('a')
which parses without errors
